  "nl_text": "This schema is for a website that allows users to create and share news articles, as well as advertisements. The database is called \"rschema_0436\" and it contains nine tables.\nThe first table, \"administrator,\" stores information about the website administrators, including their username, password, email, and creation and update timestamps. The primary key is the administrator ID.\nThe second table, \"news_article,\" stores information about the news articles, including the article title, content, author, publish time, category, and tags. The primary key is the article ID.\nThe third table, \"website_information,\" stores information about the website, including the website name, logo, copyright information, and creation and update timestamps. The primary key is the website ID.\nThe fourth table, \"member,\" stores information about the website members, including their username, password, email, and registration and update timestamps. The primary key is the member ID.\nThe fifth table, \"advertisement,\" stores information about the advertisements, including the advertisement title, content, publish time, and creation and update timestamps. The primary key is the advertisement ID.\nThe sixth table, \"message,\" stores information about the messages left by members on the news articles, including the message content, time, and reply content and time. The primary key is the message ID, and it has foreign keys to the member ID and article ID.\nThe seventh table, \"administrator_publish_news,\" stores information about which administrators have published which news articles. The primary key is the administrator ID and article ID, and it has foreign keys to the administrator ID and article ID.\nThe eighth table, \"administrator_manage_advertisement,\" stores information about which administrators have managed which advertisements. The primary key is the administrator ID and advertisement ID, and it has foreign keys to the administrator ID and advertisement ID.\nThe ninth table, \"user_message,\" stores information about which members have left messages on which news articles. The primary key is the member ID and message ID, and it has foreign keys to the member ID and message ID.\nIn summary, this schema is for a website that allows users to create and share news articles, as well as advertisements. It stores information about the website administrators, members, and advertisements, as well as the messages left by members on the news articles. The schema also includes foreign keys to ensure referential integrity and to enforce entity integrity.",
  "gold_question": "Functional description of news management system\n\nAdministrator functions:\n1. News article entry: Administrators can enter new news articles, including title, content, author, release time, etc.\n2. News article management: Administrators can edit, delete, classify, label, and other operations on published news articles.\n3. Website information settings: Administrators can set basic information of the website, such as name, logo, copyright information, etc.\n4. Member information management: Administrators can manage the website's member information, including adding, deleting, modifying, and checking member information.\n5. Advertising management: Administrators can publish, edit, and delete advertisements.\n6. Message management: Administrators can view, reply to, and delete users' messages under news articles.\n\nUser functions:\n1. Registration and login: Users can access the website through the registration and login functions.\n2. News information query: Users can query and view news information on the website.\n3. Post comments: Users can post comments under news articles.\n4. Search function: Users can search for specific news articles through the search function.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_codellama13b/0436.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0436\nCREATE TABLE `administrator` (\n  `administrator_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `username` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`administrator_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `news_article` (\n  `article_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `content` text NOT NULL,\n  `author` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `publish_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `category` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `tags` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`article_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`author`) REFERENCES `administrator` (`administrator_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `website_information` (\n  `website_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `website_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `logo` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `copyright_info` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`website_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `member` (\n  `member_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `username` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `registered_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`member_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `advertisement` (\n  `advertisement_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `content` text NOT NULL,\n  `publish_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`advertisement_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `message` (\n  `message_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `content` text NOT NULL,\n  `time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `reply_content` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `reply_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `member_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `article_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`message_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`member_id`) REFERENCES `member` (`member_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`article_id`) REFERENCES `news_article` (`article_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `administrator_publish_news` (\n  `administrator_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `article_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`administrator_id`, `article_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`administrator_id`) REFERENCES `administrator` (`administrator_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`article_id`) REFERENCES `news_article` (`article_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `administrator_manage_advertisement` (\n  `administrator_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `advertisement_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`administrator_id`, `advertisement_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`administrator_id`) REFERENCES `administrator` (`administrator_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`advertisement_id`) REFERENCES `advertisement` (`advertisement_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `user_message` (\n  `member_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `message_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`member_id`, `message_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`member_id`) REFERENCES `member` (`member_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`message_id`) REFERENCES `message` (`message_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
